{"text": "\u2018Date of Report: . February 17, 1959\n\nSeveral new liquids have been evaluated as selective\nfilter additives without an increase in the reduction over\nthat of the previously evaluated additives. \u201cIt was noted\nthat the addition of solids dissolved in solutions to paper\nplugs increases the firmness of the plug. This may be a ~!\nway to increase the machineability of paper plugs. Several 8\ncarbon filters have also been evaluated. Columbia Land +s\n4 ,, Darco KB are still superior to the others in the reduction\nP of the gas phase. Wi%ss.* \u2018 2 \u2018\n\nated for\n\u2018synthetic gas phase reduction, The concentration of TEG\non the plugs varied from 15% to 57%. It was found that\nan increase in TEG caused an increase in the reduction of\nthe methanol in the gas phase (Table I). | 7-. 05.) .\n\nGlycerine and propylene glycol were evaluated for the\nreduction of methanol. Neither showed improvement over TEC.\n\n\u201cPaper plugs containing sodium bisulfite, cysteine\njhydrochioride, or polystyrene latex became very firm when\nthey were dried. This may be a good method for increasing\nhe machineability of making paper plugs. It is felt\nhat the addition of any solid to paper plugs will increase\nheir firmness once the solids have dried on the paper. .\n\nSeveral new carbons have been evaluated along with\nColumbia L and Darco KB. Their results for gas phase\nreduction are shown in Table II.\n\n* III, Conclusions ~\n\n69ZE06TOOT\n\n\u00bb 1, An increase of TEG on a filter increases the\nreduction of methanol in the synthetic gas mixtur\n\n- 2, The addition of solids to paper filter plugs \u2018inoreases\nthe firmness of the paper.",
